{"translation": "But it is in accordance with the quality of the foods that have been taken and [209] the order of their administration that it comes about that they either float on top or pass downward.

Infant children should be bathed in warm water for a long time, and should be given wine to drink that is most watery and not altogether cold; and one should give that wine which least makes the belly rise up and produces flatulence. These things are to be done so that convulsions may seize them less and they may become larger and better-coloured.

He himself has stated the aims of the regimen described: namely, so that convulsions may seize them less and they may become larger and better-coloured. For children bathed in warm water for a longer time remain soft, and this contributes to their not being seized by convulsions.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 3, "surface": "ποιότητα", "english": "quality", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 10, "surface": "προσφορᾶς,", "english": "administration", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 25, "surface": "νήπια", "english": "infant", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 35, "surface": "ὑδαρέστατον", "english": "most watery", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": []}
